ed, but these are the intended deliverable artifacts of the task (a served local repo) rather than unwanted leftovers, so this is not penalized. The restart used a backgrounded nohup+disown pattern to return the acknowledgement before disconnect, which is a reasonable low-risk technique.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-d2d0d6beba3949f9\",\"cmd-9119252d095b47c7\",\"cmd-70f0c5c689f846ad\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets\",\"global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"All three material outcomes of the task are directly and coherently satisfied: a local RPM repository was built from local storage and served over HTTP, the remote CDN repos were made genuinely unreachable (verified via curl failures and repolist), and the tree package was installed and verified functional using only the local repo while the remote repos were unreachable. The executor also restored remote connectivity and repo configuration afterward and cleanly executed the independent node-restart evidence protocol: a healthy pre-restart baseline (boot_id, tree installed, repo HTTP 200) was captured, systemctl reboot was acknowledged, the node was observed unreachable and then recovered, boot_id changed post-restart (matching the fixed lifecycle snapshots exactly), and the installed package plus repo files/config were confirmed intact after reboot.\",\"limitations\":[\"The post-restart verification (cmd-a2cf9916a17040b7) confirmed the repo files and repo configuration persisted, but did not re-curl the HTTP endpoint to confirm the python http.server was still serving after reboot; since it was launched as an ad hoc background process rather than a systemd service, it likely did not survive the reboot. This does not affect the task's material outcomes since persistence of the HTTP service across reboot was not part of the task contract, but it is a gap in the restart evidence for that specific component.\",\"Evidence is l